※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4562

※ A reference is only worth having while what it names is still there, and a
※ caller cannot see into the function to work that out.  So a function handing
※ one back says which of the two lifetimes it is: as long as the program, which
※ `static` in the type says, or as long as whatever else in the signature carries
※ the same lifetime name.  Saying neither leaves the caller with a reference it
※ has no rule for, and that is what is refused here -- `fn first(a: &mut ⧖x u8) →
※ &mut ⧖x u8` is the program meant.
※
※ C and Odin do not ask the question at all, and a reference to a local that has
※ gone is a program nobody notices is wrong.  Rust asks it with a named lifetime
※ and lets the common case go unwritten, which is the one thing not done here:
※ there is no default, because the two answers differ and guessing either would
※ make a promise the program did not.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn first(a: &mut u8) → &mut u8:
    a
